cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >
  If you use this software or the accompanying preprint in your research,
  please cite the Zenodo preprint below. Commercial use requires a separate
  license (see COMMERCIAL.md).
type: software
title: >
  KDF: A Deterministic Architecture for Finite-Resource Information
  Preservation—Cross-Domain Evidence and Self-Refutation of Canonical Values
abstract: >
  KDF (Knowledge Decay Framework) is a deterministic graph-compression
  technique for finite-resource information preservation. Selected nodes are
  preserved verbatim while unselected nodes are discarded, distinguishing KDF
  from content-transforming methods such as LLM-based fact extraction.
  Cross-domain evidence from 100+ verified findings (F-001 -- F-100) including
  the Phase 2 / Phase 2.5 7-pattern empirical arc (5 narrowing findings + 2
  positive cross-domain replications). Streaming benefit narrows to recurring
  rare events on web access log + HW kernel log family; FAILS on distributed
  file system log domain (F-100 HDFS). Includes transparent self-refutation of
  the patent's canonical values across four benchmarks. A Reproducibility
  Guide (REPRODUCIBILITY.md) provides single-index mapping of all F-xxx
  findings to reproducer code, run commands, and expected output.
